// A generic enum's tuple variant, built with the wrong number of arguments and no
// annotation to pin its type parameter. Both compilers refuse; what is pinned here
// is WHICH question they answer first.
//
// The reference checks the count against a variant it has already resolved, so it
// fails at the inference and never reaches the count. The port counted first, and
// answered a question the reader is not stuck on: `E::P(1, 2)` with no annotation
// cannot be built whatever its arity.
//
// Two halves were needed and neither works alone - inference had to REFUSE on a
// mismatched arity instead of binding from whichever argument happened to line up,
// and only then does the order of the two calls become observable.
enum E<T> { P(T) | Q }
fn main() {
    let e = E::P(1, 2);
    println(1);
}
